1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,362
Hello!

2
00:00:00,362 --> 00:00:03,195
Today we spend a day at Hyeopjae Beach in Jeju.

3
00:00:03,195 --> 00:00:08,740
From waking up in the morning to falling asleep at night — let's describe our day in Korean!

4
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:15,646
'일과' means daily routine — the things you do every day.

5
00:00:15,646 --> 00:00:17,700
Today we learn them in order.

6
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:20,252
Key word!

7
00:00:20,252 --> 00:00:23,670
'일어나다' — to wake up, to get up from sleep.

8
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:30,736
'아침에 일어나요' (I wake up in the morning).

9
00:00:30,736 --> 00:00:35,672
'-에' marks time: 아침에 (in the morning),

10
00:00:35,672 --> 00:00:37,490
밤에 (at night).

11
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:41,838
Ask '몇 시에 일어나요'? (What time do you get up? ).

12
00:00:41,838 --> 00:00:44,350
A great conversation starter.

13
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:48,117
'일곱 시에 일어나요' (I get up at seven).

14
00:00:48,117 --> 00:00:50,170
Answer with the time.

15
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:55,177
'일찍' (early) and '늦게' (late).

16
00:00:55,177 --> 00:01:00,990
Learn them as a pair: 일찍 일어나요, 늦게 자요.

17
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:04,063
'창문을 열어요' (I open the window).

18
00:01:04,063 --> 00:01:06,259
Wow — the sea at Hyeopjae!

19
00:01:06,259 --> 00:01:08,370
And what a beautiful sky.

20
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:14,910
'세수하다' means to wash your face — the first thing in the morning.

21
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:20,062
'이를 닦다' (to brush your teeth).

22
00:01:20,062 --> 00:01:22,830
Clean them well with a toothbrush.

23
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:26,247
'옷을 입다' (to put on clothes).

24
00:01:26,247 --> 00:01:30,274
The opposite is '옷을 벗다' (to take off).

25
00:01:30,274 --> 00:01:32,500
Learn them as a pair.

26
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:43,790
Use these to connect your routine: '세수하고 그다음에 아침을 먹어요' (I wash my face and then eat breakfast).

27
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:49,326
'아침을 먹다' (to eat breakfast).

28
00:01:49,326 --> 00:01:55,380
아침/점심/저녁 + 먹다 — three meals a day.

29
00:01:55,830 --> 00:01:58,980
'마시다' is for liquids.

30
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:06,030
Korean distinguishes 먹다 (eat) from 마시다 (drink).

31
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,633
Uh-oh — look at the clock, we're late!

32
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:10,390
And we have to get to the cafe!

33
00:02:13,410 --> 00:02:18,160
'서두르다' means to hurry. Let's move quickly!

34
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:23,030
Key word! '일하다' — to work.

35
00:02:26,410 --> 00:02:34,328
'-에서' marks the place where an action happens: 카페에서 (at a cafe),

36
00:02:34,328 --> 00:02:36,060
집에서 (at home).

37
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:40,903
Key word! '공부하다' — to study.

38
00:02:40,903 --> 00:02:43,200
I study Korean every day.

39
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:52,474
'을/를' marks the object: 한국어를 공부해요 (I study Korean),

40
00:02:52,474 --> 00:02:55,890
책을 읽어요 (I read a book).

41
00:02:56,340 --> 00:03:01,120
'책을 읽다' (to read a book). Sit quietly and read.

42
00:03:01,570 --> 00:03:06,060
'쓰다' means to write. Write it in your notebook.

43
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:10,462
'점심을 먹다' (to eat lunch).

44
00:03:10,462 --> 00:03:14,300
Eating lunch with a sea view — the best!

45
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:18,363
Wait — look at the sky! The clouds are beautiful.

46
00:03:18,363 --> 00:03:20,250
I really love watching clouds.

47
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:29,340
'쉬다' means to rest — to stop working and relax.

48
00:03:32,110 --> 00:03:35,969
'일하고 나서 쉬어요' (I rest after working).

49
00:03:35,969 --> 00:03:39,720
'-고 나서' shows the order of actions.

50
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:43,258
'산책하다' — to take a leisurely walk.

51
00:03:43,258 --> 00:03:45,710
Let's walk along the beach.

52
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:50,188
'해변을 걸어요' (I walk on the beach).

53
00:03:50,188 --> 00:03:53,510
The white sand at Hyeopjae is so soft.

54
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:59,020
'수영하다' (to swim). Let's swim in the emerald water!

55
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:01,944
'사진을 찍다' (to take a photo).

56
00:04:01,944 --> 00:04:05,610
One shot with Biyangdo Island behind us!

57
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:10,450
'재미있어요'! (It's fun! ). What a great day.

58
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:15,940
After all that play — '피곤해요' (I'm tired).

59
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:21,820
'저녁을 먹다' (to eat dinner) — the last meal of the day.

60
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:26,467
'자기 전에 씻어요' (I wash before sleeping).

61
00:04:26,467 --> 00:04:30,210
'-기 전에' = before doing something.

62
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:35,181
'저녁을 먹은 후에 산책해요' (I walk after dinner).

63
00:04:35,181 --> 00:04:38,890
'-ㄴ 후에' = after doing something.

64
00:04:39,340 --> 00:04:40,843
'전화하다' (to call).

65
00:04:40,843 --> 00:04:44,910
Call your family and tell them about your day.

66
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:53,470
'보다' means to watch/see. 텔레비전을 봐요 (I watch TV).

67
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,534
My favorite moment!

68
00:04:55,534 --> 00:04:58,167
'노을을 보다' (to watch the sunset).

69
00:04:58,167 --> 00:05:01,140
The sunset at Hyeopjae is stunning.

70
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:04,355
'아름다워요' (It's beautiful).

71
00:05:04,355 --> 00:05:06,920
The whole sky turns orange.

72
00:05:09,390 --> 00:05:14,410
'샤워하다' (to take a shower). Wash up before bed.

73
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:17,165
'일기를 쓰다' (to write a diary).

74
00:05:17,165 --> 00:05:20,210
Write about your day — a great habit.

75
00:05:21,570 --> 00:05:23,872
Wait — the sky is full of stars!

76
00:05:23,872 --> 00:05:25,670
I really love stargazing.

77
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:31,600
Sitting on the sand, watching the stars.

78
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:33,350
What a lovely day it's been.

79
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:38,860
Key word! '자다' — to sleep. The end of the day.

80
00:05:40,010 --> 00:05:45,557
'열한 시에 자요' (I sleep at eleven). Time + 자다.

81
00:05:45,557 --> 00:05:47,670
The day is done.

82
00:05:48,430 --> 00:05:53,881
Let's review: 일어나다 (wake up), 일하다 (work),

83
00:05:53,881 --> 00:05:59,864
공부하다 (study), 자다 (sleep). How about your day?

84
00:05:59,864 --> 00:06:04,650
Tell us in the comments. Good night!

